-- title:
--   The focal subgroup theorem
-- statement:
--   **The focal subgroup theorem.**
--
--   For a Sylow $p$-subgroup $P$ of finite index in a group $G$,
--   $$[G,G] \cap P \;=\; \operatorname{Foc}_G(P),$$
--   where the focal subgroup is generated by the elements $x^{-1}y$ for $x, y \in P$ conjugate in
--   $G$.
--
--   The left side is global — it asks which elements of $P$ lie in the commutator subgroup of the
--   whole group — while the right side is visible from the fusion data alone, that is, from
--   knowing which elements of $P$ become conjugate in $G$. The theorem says these coincide, so a
--   purely local computation inside $P$ together with its fusion determines a global invariant.
--
--   This is the engine behind transfer arguments in finite group theory. Combined with the
--   transfer homomorphism it yields Burnside's normal $p$-complement theorem and, ultimately,
--   $p$-nilpotence criteria of Frobenius and Thompson; the modern formulation in terms of fusion
--   systems takes the focal subgroup as a basic construction.
--
--   Due to Higman (1953), building on Grün's earlier transfer theorems.
--
--   **Formalization note.** `Sylow p G` is the type of Sylow `p`-subgroups, `P.FiniteIndex` is the
--   finite-index hypothesis, and `commutator G` is the derived subgroup. The result is Mathlib's
--   `Subgroup.commutator_inf_eq_focalSubgroup`.
-- source:
--   Listed in Mathlib's "1000 theorems" manifest (docs/1000.yaml); formalized in Mathlib. Proof here reduces to the corresponding Mathlib result.

import Mathlib

namespace FamousTheorems

universe u v

open Filter Set Topology DirectSum

theorem focal_subgroup {G : Type*} [Group G] {p : ℕ} [Fact p.Prime] (P : Sylow p G)
    [P.FiniteIndex] : commutator G ⊓ (P : Subgroup G) = P.focalSubgroup := by sorry

end FamousTheorems
